(Conway's Game of Life on a board whose edges wrap around, so a glider
 that leaves on one side comes back on the other)

A number called width is 40.
A number called height is 20.
A number called generations is 14.

A buffer called grid is 800 bytes in size.
A buffer called 'next grid' is 800 bytes in size.

(Step off one edge of the board and come back on at the opposite edge)
To wrap with a number called position and a number called step and a number called 'board size'.
    Return a number, {{{position subtract 1} add step add 'board size'} modulo 'board size'} add 1.

To 'cell index' with a number called row and a number called column.
    Return a number, {{row subtract 1} multiply width} add column.

To 'bring to life' with a number called row and a number called column.
    Set byte {'cell index' of row and column} of grid to 1.

(A living cell holds 1 and an empty one 0, so adding up states counts the living)
To 'the state at' with a number called row and a number called column.
    Return a number, byte {'cell index' of row and column} of grid.

(The three-by-three square around a cell holds its eight neighbours and the
 cell itself, so the cell's own state comes off the total)
To 'alive neighbors' with a number called row and a number called column.
    A number called tally is 0.
    For each 'row step' from -1 to 1,
        a number called 'neighbor row' is wrap of row and 'row step' and height,
        For each 'column step' from -1 to 1,
            a number called 'neighbor column' is wrap of column and 'column step' and width,
            the tally is tally add 'the state at' of 'neighbor row' and 'neighbor column'..
    Return a number, tally subtract 'the state at' of row and column.

(A row is built up in one buffer and printed once, so it comes out as one line)
To 'print row' with a number called row.
    A buffer called line is 64 bytes in size.
    For each column from 1 to width,
        a text called mark is ".",
        If 'the state at' of row and column is 1 then, the mark is "#".
        Append mark to line.
    Print line.

(Every cell's next state is worked out from the current grid alone, into
 the next grid, so the cells can be stepped in any order)
To 'step cell' with a number called row and a number called column.
    A number called neighbors is 'alive neighbors' of row and column.
    A number called state is 'the state at' of row and column.
    A number called 'next state' is 0.
    If state is 1 and neighbors is 2 then, the 'next state' is 1.
    If state is 1 and neighbors is 3 then, the 'next state' is 1.
    If state is 0 and neighbors is 3 then, the 'next state' is 1.
    Set byte {'cell index' of row and column} of 'next grid' to 'next state'.

To 'run generation' with a number called generation.
    Print "Generation {generation} of {generations}:".
    'print row' of each row from 1 to height.
    'step cell' of each row from 1 to height and each column from 1 to width.
    Copy 'next grid' to grid.

(An R-pentomino: five cells that stay restless for about 1100 generations
 on a board without edges)
'bring to life' of 9 and 20.
'bring to life' of 9 and 21.
'bring to life' of 10 and 19.
'bring to life' of 10 and 20.
'bring to life' of 11 and 20.

(A glider, which drifts diagonally away from the top corner)
'bring to life' of 2 and 3.
'bring to life' of 3 and 4.
'bring to life' of 4 and 2.
'bring to life' of 4 and 3.
'bring to life' of 4 and 4.

Print "Conway's Game of Life on a 40 by 20 board whose edges wrap, seeded with an R-pentomino and a glider".

Create a timer called clock.
Start the clock.

'run generation' of each generation from 1 to generations.

Stop the clock.
A number called 'time taken' is the clock's duration in milliseconds.
Print "Ran {generations} generations on an 800-cell grid in {time taken} milliseconds, as native machine code.".
